guide_html: |-
  <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-slate-950">Pakistan's five 8,000-metre peaks</h2>
  <ul>
  <li><strong><a href="/expeditions/k2-expedition/">K2</a></strong>, 8,611 m: the second-highest mountain in the world, climbed from the Godwin-Austen Glacier.</li>
  <li><strong><a href="/expeditions/nanga-parbat-expedition/">Nanga Parbat</a></strong>, 8,126 m: the western end of the Himalaya, approached from its own valley.</li>
  <li><strong><a href="/expeditions/gasherbrum-i-expedition/">Gasherbrum I</a></strong>, 8,080 m: also called Hidden Peak.</li>
  <li><strong><a href="/expeditions/broad-peak-expedition/">Broad Peak</a></strong>, 8,051 m: the peak most often paired with K2.</li>
  <li><strong><a href="/expeditions/gasherbrum-ii-expedition/">Gasherbrum II</a></strong>, 8,035 m: often chosen as a first 8,000-metre peak.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>K2, Broad Peak and the two Gasherbrums share the Baltoro Glacier approach and the same climbing season in summer. The royalty is USD 3,500 for K2 and USD 2,500 for each of the others; see our <a href="/blog/pakistan-mountaineering-royalty-fees/">royalty fee guide</a>. Trekkers can visit four of these base camps on the <a href="/trekking/k2-base-camp-trek/">K2 Base Camp Trek</a> route.</p>
